course: dictionary
lesson: 3676
title: Fizzle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Fizzle has two meanings, and context tells you which one you need. When something makes a soft hissing or spitting sound, like a sparkler or a fizzy drink, it fizzles. «You don't have to join every contest; stepping out often makes a rivalry fizzle.» Here the rivalry is not hissing, so fizzle must mean something else.
explain: In this sentence, a rivalry is a competition between people who want to beat each other. If stepping out makes it fizzle, the rivalry slowly ends in a weak way, with no big finish. This is the informal meaning: something starts well, then fades away and is disappointing.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. «Stepping out often makes a rivalry slowly end in a weak way» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Phrases like «fizzle out» and «end in a fizzle» usually show this second meaning.
fact: Fizzle means to make a soft hissing or spitting sound.
fact: «Fizzle out» means to slowly end in a weak, disappointing way after a good start.
fact: A fizzle, as an informal noun, is a disappointing failure.
fact: Fizzling happens in science when a gas escapes from a liquid, or when a fuse burns.
fact: Plans, storms, friendships and excitement can all fizzle out in everyday life and in stories.
exercise: «The sparkler began to fizzle in the dark garden.» What does fizzle mean here? | Fall fast asleep | Grow very large | *Make a soft hissing, spitting sound
exercise: Mia's club started with a bang, but by summer the plans had fizzled out. What does fizzled out mean? | *Slowly ended in a weak, disappointing way | Was painted gold | Got bigger and better each week
exercise: «The team's excitement turned into a fizzle after one match.» What is a fizzle in this sentence? | A loud celebration | A brand new football | *A disappointing failure
test: «Stepping out often makes a rivalry fizzle.» What happens to the rivalry? | *Slowly ends in a weak way | Turns into a prize | Gets louder and angrier
test: Which phrase means a plan ended in a disappointing way? | Start with a bang | Last forever | *End in a fizzle
exam: Which sentence uses fizzle to mean a disappointing ending? | The fizzy drink fizzled in the glass. | The dog barked at the post. | *The plan fizzled out after one week.
